1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,699
Rearrange an array into three groups, in
place, in a single pass. It looks easy.

2
00:00:05,749 --> 00:00:08,430
But most people get the indices wrong.

3
00:00:09,467 --> 00:00:11,936
The Dutch national flag problem.

4
00:00:11,986 --> 00:00:15,321
You are given an array,
A, and an index, i.

5
00:00:15,371 --> 00:00:19,337
Rearrange the array around
the value at that index.

6
00:00:19,867 --> 00:00:23,897
Here, i is three, so the
pivot value is three.

7
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:29,452
The goal: every smaller value first,
then the values equal to the pivot,

8
00:00:29,502 --> 00:00:30,931
then the larger ones.

9
00:00:30,981 --> 00:00:34,943
And it should take linear
time, and constant extra space.

10
00:00:35,967 --> 00:00:41,581
The brute force way is to copy the
values into three lists: lower, equal,

11
00:00:41,631 --> 00:00:43,677
and higher. Then join them.

12
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:47,719
That is correct, but the
three lists cost extra space,

13
00:00:47,769 --> 00:00:50,230
proportional to the size of the array.

14
00:00:51,367 --> 00:00:57,026
A better idea is to keep four regions
inside the same array: less than the pivot,

15
00:00:57,076 --> 00:00:59,423
equal to it, unknown, and greater.

16
00:00:59,473 --> 00:01:04,357
Three indices, called smaller, equal,
and larger, mark the boundaries.

17
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:07,761
Each step classifies one unknown element,

18
00:01:07,811 --> 00:01:11,170
so the unknown region
shrinks until it is empty.

19
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:16,927
From here, only the pivot's value matters.

20
00:01:16,977 --> 00:01:19,725
Smaller and equal both start at zero.

21
00:01:19,775 --> 00:01:22,070
Larger starts one past the end.

22
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:26,955
The element at equal is
three, the same as the pivot.

23
00:01:27,005 --> 00:01:31,010
It extends the equal block, so
equal moves one step right.

24
00:01:32,133 --> 00:01:35,402
Next is five, which is
greater than the pivot.

25
00:01:35,452 --> 00:01:40,163
Move larger one step left, then
swap the five into the top region.

26
00:01:40,713 --> 00:01:42,836
Notice that equal does not move.

27
00:01:42,886 --> 00:01:46,503
The two that just arrived
has not been classified yet.

28
00:01:47,753 --> 00:01:52,263
Two is less than the pivot, so swap
it with the element at smaller.

29
00:01:53,613 --> 00:01:58,631
The three that came back is a pivot
value, so it is already classified.

30
00:01:58,681 --> 00:02:00,843
Both smaller and equal advance.

31
00:02:01,993 --> 00:02:06,903
Two again: less than the
pivot. Swap, and advance both.

32
00:02:08,053 --> 00:02:11,443
Three equals the pivot. Equal moves right.

33
00:02:12,587 --> 00:02:14,659
One is less than the pivot.

34
00:02:14,709 --> 00:02:17,097
Swap it down, and advance both.

35
00:02:18,247 --> 00:02:23,717
Five is greater. Move larger
left, and swap. Equal stays put.

36
00:02:24,867 --> 00:02:29,377
The three that arrived equals
the pivot, so equal moves right.

37
00:02:29,827 --> 00:02:31,856
Equal has now reached larger.

38
00:02:31,906 --> 00:02:35,297
No unknown elements are
left, and the loop ends.

39
00:02:36,247 --> 00:02:39,024
One pass over the array is linear time.

40
00:02:39,074 --> 00:02:43,157
And three indices, with no
extra list, is constant space.

41
00:02:44,807 --> 00:02:47,380
So, where is this used? In quicksort.

42
00:02:47,430 --> 00:02:53,195
After one partition, every value equal to
the pivot is already in its final place.

43
00:02:53,245 --> 00:02:57,237
Only the smaller and the larger
parts still need sorting.

44
00:02:57,787 --> 00:03:02,407
A plain two-way partition would sort
those equal values again and again.

45
00:03:02,457 --> 00:03:07,337
That is why a three-way partition is
used when the data has many duplicates.

46
00:03:07,887 --> 00:03:12,113
The same step also finds the
median, or the k-th smallest value,

47
00:03:12,163 --> 00:03:13,917
without sorting everything.

48
00:03:14,967 --> 00:03:17,063
And why do interviewers ask it?

49
00:03:17,113 --> 00:03:21,702
The idea is simple, but the index
bookkeeping is easy to get wrong.

50
00:03:21,752 --> 00:03:26,687
The fix is to state the invariant
first: four regions, three boundaries.

51
00:03:26,737 --> 00:03:30,357
Then every line of the loop
only has to keep it true.

52
00:03:30,907 --> 00:03:36,276
The same habit solves related problems,
like sort colors, move zeros to the end,

53
00:03:36,326 --> 00:03:38,377
and remove duplicates in place.

54
00:03:40,427 --> 00:03:41,671
Thanks for watching.

55
00:03:41,721 --> 00:03:46,267
Subscribe to Edge Cases for more
Python, algorithms, and system design,

56
00:03:46,317 --> 00:03:47,497
explained visually.
